## Results

Both reports say `status=loaded`, `acceptance=counted`, `guest_started=true`,
and exact nonce marker present. Both stopped at `retired=2,999,999,998` with
`budget_exhausted`, `cpl3_retired=873`, and one delivered `#GP(0)`. Schema-v2
sidecars captured all 34 successfully delivered #PF events in each run with
zero omitted records.

| Run | Nonce | RDTSC reads | #PF | IRQ0 edges / delivered | Quanta | Halt idle ns | Virtual ns |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| `i` | `od5ctx20261115i` | 34,431,640 | 34 | 34,787 / 34,792 | 34,558,323 | 1,090,791 | 37,449,633,789 |
| `j` | `od5ctx20261115j` | 34,461,714 | 34 | 34,817 / 34,822 | 34,587,372 | 1,130,225 | 37,479,747,223 |

In both runs, event index 30 is the same kernel byte-store instruction and
fault shape:

| Field | Run `i` | Run `j` |
|---|---|---|
| RIP | `0xffffffff8271a9c1` | `0xffffffff8271a9c1` |
| Symbol/instruction | `rep_movs_alternative+0x11`, `MOVB AL,(RDI)` | same |
| Access / CPL / error | Write / 0 / `0x00000002` | same |
| CR2 = RDI | `0x7fff67cbc909` | `0x7fff160bdeb9` |
| RCX | `0x7` | `0x7` |
| RSI | `0xffffffff82fdeb8f` | `0xffffffff82fdeb8f` |
| Kernel RSP / RBP | `0xffffc9000000fc80` / `0xffffc9000000fc88` | same |
| CR3 | `0x6474000` | `0x64b0000` |

The next recorded #PF in both sidecars is the CPL3 instruction-fetch fault at
`RIP=CR2=0x4000b0`; its user RSP is `0x7fff67cbc750` in `i` and
`0x7fff160bdd00` in `j`. In both runs, the faulting destination is exactly
`0x1b9` bytes above that later user RSP. The captured fault is one byte store;
`RCX=7` and the matching copy-loop disassembly are consistent with seven
remaining bytes being copied to an address near the later user stack. Since
the sidecar records #PF events rather than a complete instruction trace, it
does not establish how much execution occurs between these two faults. The
kernel and user CR3 values differ by `0x1000` in each run.

The faulting source address `RSI` resolves inside `.rodata` of the matching
debug kernel. Reading the ELF section bytes there yields
`x86_64\0i386\0r14\0r13...`; `RCX=7` is consistent with copying the seven
bytes of `x86_64` plus its terminating NUL. This is consistent with a
startup-stack platform-string copy, but the bytes alone do not prove the
higher-level caller or its purpose. Error code `0x2` records a supervisor
write to a not-present page. A demand/first-touch explanation is plausible:
the event is successfully delivered, the guest reaches CPL3 in both runs, and
the exact relative destination offset matches. The pair does not capture the
PTE before/after delivery, so it does not prove why the page was absent. The
previous schema-v1 pair had 33 faults in `g` and 34 in `h`, so event occurrence
still varies across the broader run set.

The CPL0 RDTSC totals differ by 30,074 between `i` and `j`; IRQ0 edges and
quanta also differ. No comparator ran for these single reports, no difference
is classified benign, no normalization changed, and S5-07 remains
blocked/unaccepted. The register snapshot narrows the #PF operation but does
not explain its run-to-run occurrence or the remaining timing/UART variation.

